Let $(A, \Phi)$ be an $L_1^2$ solution to \eqref{hitchin-sd} then $\|F_A\|_{L^2} = \|[\Phi, \Phi^*]\|_{L^2} < \infty.$ Take a local chart $(U, z)$ with a given local frame of $V$ such that $\|F_A|_U\|_{L^2}$ is sufficiently small. We express $A = \mathrm{d} + 	heta, ~ \Phi = \phi \, \mathrm{d}z,$ where $	heta$ is a local skew-Hermitian 1-form and $\phi$ is a locally matrix-valued function. The self-duality equations can then be written locally as:  egin{align*} &	ext{d}	heta + 	heta \wedge 	heta + [\phi, \phi^{\dagger_H}] \, 	ext{d}z \wedge 	ext{d} ar{z} = -\pi i \deg(V) \, 	ext{id}_V \, \omega_M, \ & ar{\partial} \phi + [	heta^{0,1}, \phi] = 0. \end{align*} Consider the $L_2^2$ unitary gauge transformation $g$ on $V|_U$ provided by Coulomb gauge Theorem ef{U-reg}, and let $(	ilde{A} = 	ext{d} + 	ilde{	heta}, 	ilde{\Phi} = 	ilde{\phi} \, 	ext{d}z) := (A, \Phi) \cdot g$. This gauge-transformed pair naturally satisfies the self-duality equation \eqref{hitchin-sd}, leading to the following coupled system of elliptic equations:  egin{align*} &\Delta 	ilde{	heta} + 	ext{d}^* \Big(	ilde{	heta} \wedge 	ilde{	heta} + [	ilde{\phi}, 	ilde{\phi}^{\dagger_H}] \, 	ext{d}z \wedge 	ext{d} ar{z}\Big) = 0, \ & ar{\partial} 	ilde{\phi} + [	ilde{	heta}^{0,1}, 	ilde{\phi}] = 0. \end{align*} By Corollary~ef{SobM}, since $ ar{\partial} 	ilde{\phi} = -[	ilde{	heta}^{0,1}, 	ilde{\phi}]$ has $L_1^{2 - \varepsilon}$ regularity, applying elliptic estimates again implies $L_2^{2 - \varepsilon}$ regularity for $	ilde{\phi}$. Similarly, $\Delta 	ilde{	heta} = -*\mathrm{d}*\Big(	ilde{	heta} \wedge 	ilde{	heta} + [	ilde{\phi}, 	ilde{\phi}^{\dagger_H}] \, \mathrm{d}z \wedge \mathrm{d} ar{z}\Big)$ has $L^{2 - \varepsilon}$ regularity. Using elliptic estimates, it follows that $	ilde{	heta}$ has $L_2^{2 - \varepsilon}$ regularity. Repeating this iterative process successively improves the regularity of $	ilde{	heta}$ and $	ilde{\phi}$. Thus, we conclude that $(	ilde{A}, 	ilde{\Phi})$ is smooth in $U$. Let $ ig\{(U_\alpha, z_\alpha) ig\}_{\alpha=1}^n$ be an atlas of a Riemann surface $M$, such that each $U_\alpha$ is diffeomorphic to a disk in $\mathbb{C}$. Let $V_\alpha := V ig|_{U_\alpha}$, and suppose there exists an $L_2^2$ unitary gauge transformation $g_\alpha$ of $V_\alpha$ such that the pair $(A_\alpha, \Phi_\alpha) := (A, \Phi) ig|_{U_\alpha} \cdot g_\alpha$ is a smooth pair on $U_\alpha$. Since the space of smooth unitary gauge transformations is dense in the space of $L_2^2$ unitary gauge transformations on $(V_\alpha, H)$, there exists a smooth unitary gauge transformation $s_\alpha$ on $V_\alpha$ such that $\|g_\alpha^{-1} - s_\alpha\|_{L_2^2} < \varepsilon_1$ for any fixed small positive number $\varepsilon_1$. Let $\mathrm{id}_\alpha$ denote the identity map on $V_\alpha$. Then, \[ \|\mathrm{id}_\alpha - g_\alpha s_\alpha\|_{L_2^2} \leq C_\alpha \|g_\alpha\|_{L_2^2} \left\|g_\alpha^{-1} - s_\alphaight\|_{L_2^2}, \] where $C_\alpha$ depends only on $U_\alpha$. Hence, we can make an assumption that \[ \|g_\alpha - \mathrm{id}_\alpha\|_{L_2^2} < \varepsilon_2 \quad 	ext{for each } \alpha = 1, \cdots, n, \] where $\varepsilon_2$ is a fixed small positive number. Denote $V_{\alpha eta} := V|_{U_\alpha \cap U_ eta}$ for each $U_\alpha \cap U_ eta 
eq \emptyset$. Let $g_{\alpha eta} := g_ eta^{-1} \cdot g_\alpha$, then $g_{\alpha eta}$ is a gauge transformation on $V_{\alpha eta}$ that sends the smooth pair $(A_ eta, \Phi_ eta)|_{U_{\alpha eta}}$ to another smooth pair $(A_\alpha, \Phi_\alpha)|_{U_{\alpha eta}}$. By Theorem ef{A-B}, $g_{\alpha eta}$ is smooth. Moreover, we can assume that \[ \|g_{\alpha eta} - \mathrm{id}_{\alpha eta}\|_{L_2^2} < \varepsilon_3, \] where $\varepsilon_3$ is a fixed small positive number. There is an open neighborhood of $0 \in \mathfrak{su}_2$ such that $\exp : \mathfrak{su}_2 	o SU(2)$ is a diffeomorphism on it. When we use the symbols $\exp \xi$ or $\exp^{-1}s$, we mean that $\xi$ is near $0 \in \mathfrak{su}_2$ or $s$ is near $\mathrm{id} \in SU(2)$, respectively. In this case, we can suppose that $g_{\alpha eta} = \exp \eta_{\alpha eta}$, where $\eta_{\alpha eta}$ is a smooth function on $V_{\alpha eta}$ with values near $0\in\mathfrak{su}_2$. Consider the distance function $	ext{d}: M 	imes M 	o \mathbb{R}$ given by the Kähler metric on $M$, and define \[ U_\alpha^\delta =  ig\{z \in U_\alpha \ | \ 	ext{d}(z, \partial U_\alpha) < \delta ig\}. \] For $\delta$ sufficiently small, we can assume that $\{U_\alpha^{n\cdot\delta}\}_{\alpha=1}^n$ is also an open cover of $M$. Next, we will construct a global unitary gauge transformation $	ilde{g}$ such that $g_\alpha^{-1} \cdot 	ilde{g} ig|_{U_\alpha^{n\cdot\delta}}$ is smooth on each $U_\alpha^{n\cdot\delta}$ for $1 \leq \alpha \leq n$. Consequently, $(A, \Phi) \cdot 	ilde{g}$ is the smooth pair we want, since \[  ig(A, \Phi ig) \cdot 	ilde{g} = (A_\alpha, \Phi_\alpha) \cdot  ig(g_\alpha^{-1} \cdot 	ilde{g} ig) \] is smooth on each $U_\alpha^{n\cdot\delta}$. We use induction to prove the following proposition: For any $1 \leq k \leq n$, there exists a sequence of local unitary gauge transformations \[ \Big\{	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)}: V ig|_{U_\alpha^{k\cdot\delta}} 	o V ig|_{U_\alpha^{k\cdot\delta}}\Big\}_{\alpha=1}^k \] such that: $g_\alpha^{-1} 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)}$ is smooth, and $	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)} = 	ilde{g}_ eta^{(k)}$ if $U_\alpha^{k\cdot\delta} \cap U_ eta^{k\cdot\delta} 
eq \emptyset$. For $k = 1$, define $	ilde{g}_1^{(1)} = g_1^{(1)} ig|_{U_1^\delta}$. Clearly, $	ilde{g}_1^{(1)}$ satisfies the required conditions. Assume the proposition holds for $k$. Next, we prove the statement for $k+1$. Denote $\mathcal{U}_k^\varepsilon :=  igcup_{1 \leq \alpha \leq k} U_\alpha^\varepsilon$ and consider the two following cases Case 1: If $U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} \cap \mathcal{U}_k^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} = \emptyset$, define $	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k+1)} := 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)} ig|_{U_\alpha^{(k+1)\cdot\delta}}$ for $1 \leq \alpha \leq k$ and $	ilde{g}_{k+1}^{(k+1)} := g_{k+1} ig|_{U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta}}$. Clearly, this choice satisfies the required conditions. Case 2: If $U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} \cap \mathcal{U}_k^{(k+1)\delta} 
eq \emptyset$, there exists a smooth function $\phi_{k+1}: U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} 	o [0, 1]$ such that: \[ \phi_{k+1} =  egin{cases} 0, & 	ext{on } U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} \cap \mathcal{U}_k^{(k+1)\cdot\delta}, \ 1, & 	ext{on } U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} \setminus \mathcal{U}_k^{k\cdot\delta}. \end{cases} \] We define $	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k+1)} := 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)} ig|_{U_\alpha^{(k+1)\cdot\delta}}$ for $1 \leq \alpha \leq k$, and \[ 	ilde{g}_{k+1}^{(k+1)} =  egin{cases} 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)} \exp\left\{\phi_{k+1} \exp^{-1} \left\{	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)-1} g_{k+1}ight\}ight\}, & \quad 	ext{on } U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} \cap U_\alpha^{k\cdot\delta}, \ g_{k+1}, & \quad 	ext{on } U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} \setminus \mathcal{U}_k^{k\cdot\delta}. \end{cases} \] Since $	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)} = 	ilde{g}_ eta^{(k)}$ whenever $U_\alpha^{k\cdot\delta} \cap U_ eta^{k\cdot\delta} 
eq \emptyset$, this definition is well-defined. First, $	ilde{g}_{k+1}^{(k+1)} = 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k+1)}$ on $U_{k+1}^{(k+1)\cdot\delta} \cap U_\alpha^{k\cdot\delta} 
eq \emptyset$ since $\phi_{k+1} = 0$. Second, $g_{k+1}^{-1} 	ilde{g}_{k+1}^{(k+1)}$ is smooth, as required, since  egin{align*} g_{k+1}^{-1} 	ilde{g}_{k+1}^{(k+1)} &= g_{k+1}^{-1} 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)} \exp\left\{\phi_{k+1} \exp^{-1} \left\{	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)-1} g_{k+1}ight\}ight\} \ &= \left(g_{k+1}^{-1} g_\alphaight)\left(g_\alpha^{-1} 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)}ight) \exp\Big\{\phi_{k+1} \exp^{-1}\Big\{\left(g_\alpha^{-1} 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)}ight)^{-1} \left(g_\alpha^{-1} g_{k+1}ight)\Big\}\Big\}. \end{align*} We emphasize that all components in this expression, namely $g_{k+1}^{-1} g_\alpha$, $g_\alpha^{-1} 	ilde{g}_\alpha^{(k)}$, $\exp(\cdot)$, and $\phi_{k+1}$, are smooth.

